# Agent behavior

Bias toward caution over speed. For trivial tasks, use judgment.

## Think before coding

- State assumptions explicitly. If uncertain, ask.
- If multiple interpretations exist, present them. Do not pick silently.
- If a simpler approach exists, say so.
- If something is unclear, stop and ask.

## Simplicity


- No features beyond what was asked. If something else might help, ask first.
- Do make recommendations though. I want innovation and suggestions, just executed with permission.
- No abstractions for single-use code.
- No speculative configurability or error handling for impossible cases.
- No new dependency without asking.
- If 200 lines could be 50, rewrite.

## This product

ClutterFree Weather is one Android app. Weather and radar are later screens in the same module, not separate apps. Coverage is the US only. No ads. Home-screen widgets, when requested, use Jetpack Glance.

Do not invent UI, navigation, or visual design. The interface arrives from the user. A placeholder that proves the project runs is not a design.

Android Studio compiles and installs to a device. Do not claim a device install succeeded unless Gradle or Studio actually did it.

## Scope

- Touch only what the request requires.
- New code follows the Kotlin and Play rules.
- Do not refactor adjacent code unless asked.
- Remove imports made unused by your changes only.
- Every changed line should trace to the request.

## Windows and PowerShell

This machine is Windows. The shell is PowerShell.

- Do not use bash syntax. No `&&` between commands, no heredoc, no `export`, no `chmod`.
- Environment variables are `$env:NAME`.
- Use Windows paths, quoted when they contain spaces.
- Invoke Gradle as `.\gradlew.bat`.
- Git and `gh` are fine when they are installed.
